El viernes, 1 de octubre de 2021 a las 23:42:03 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> You need to use the "include" argument, like
>
> predict(..., ~ point_field_copy, include=c("point_field_copy"))
>
> The error is related to model components that cannot be evaluated using only the supplied data, so you need to use the include/exclude system to prevent it from trying to evaluate them.
>
> On Fri, 1 Oct 2021 at 23:34, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Finn,
> >
> > I have tried to plot the field components (mark and point) and in both cases I get the same error (the one that we get at the beginning).
> >
> > pxl <- pixels(mesh, mask=boundary)
> > pxl <- cprod(pxl, data.frame(ti=seq_len(1)))
> >
> > > predpoint <- predict(fit,
> > +                      pxl,
> > +                      ~ mark_field
> > +                      ,n.samples=100)
> > Error in row_kron(A[[k + 1]], A_) :
> >   Size mismatch for row.kron, (n1, n2) = (1, 0)
> > In addition: Warning message:
> > In qnorm(psn.new <http://psn.new>) : NaNs produced
> >
> > > predpoint <- predict(fit,
> > +                      pxl,
> > +                      ~ point_field_copy
> > +                      ,n.samples=100)
> > Error in row_kron(A[[k + 1]], A_) :
> >   Size mismatch for row.kron, (n1, n2) = (1, 0)
> > In addition: Warning message:
> > In qnorm(psn.new <http://psn.new>) : NaNs produced
> >
> > Thanks again for your help
> >
> > Oscar
> > El viernes, 1 de octubre de 2021 a las 9:30:30 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> > > You can plot the estimated field components of the model, but to include the estimated effect of things like “rural” in the map, you will need to know the value of “rural” in every point you’re trying to predict; the estimated coefficient by itself only scales the covariate, and doesn’t tell you what the effect is at each location.
> > >
> > > Finn
> > >
> > > > On 1 Oct 2021, at 09:14, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > ﻿Yes, but in the same way that you can obtain a "heat map" in a "simple" spatial model having only data in the points, you can also here obtain that map, working only with that points, isn't it?
> > > > Maybe I am totally wrong, I am just trying to understand how inlabru and marked point process work.
> > > > About data in the whole area, I can organise some covariates in a country level but some of them are related to the points (so those should go only with the mark) and DC is in a grid in a daily basis (and I have populated the data in years). For that reason I want to understand first the whole process and then go to the more complex problem. If you are happy we can talk about this in the future.
> > > >
> > > > Thanks for all your help and sorry again for my lack of knowledge!
> > > >
> > > > Oscar
> > > >
> > > > El viernes, 1 de octubre de 2021 a las 8:43:17 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> > > > > To predict on the whole region you must have all the covariates across that whole region.
> > > > >
> > > > > Finn
> > > > >
> > > > > > On 1 Oct 2021, at 07:40, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > >
> > > > > > ﻿Well, I need the prediction in the whole area, but at the moment I am just able to obtain the points.
> > > > > > I will try this morning running again the prediction without the plogis transformation and then try again with the whole area.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Oscar
> > > > > >
> > > > > > El jueves, 30 de septiembre de 2021 a las 23:36:29 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> > > > > > > Yes, if you only need prediction at the observed points then that should work, but it seems there are numerical issues with the model causing extreme values somewhere. Start by predicting something simpler (e.g. just covariate effects, and just the spatial fields, and without the extra plogis transformation, just to see what the estimated components look like, and start by looking at the summary() output.
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > Finn
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > On 30 Sep 2021, at 23:15, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > ﻿Hi,
> > > > > > > > So in that case, using "data" instead of grid coordinates, should work, isn't it? I have tried with the following code:
> > > > > > > > predjoint <- predict(fit,
> > > > > > > >                      data,
> > > > > > > >                      ~ plogis(Inter_mark +
> > > > > > > >                                 point_field +
> > > > > > > >                                 point_field_copy +
> > > > > > > >                                 mark_field +
> > > > > > > >                                 DC+GDP+density+total+rural+agriculture+industry +services +forest),
> > > > > > > >                      n.samples=1000)
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > and I get the following warning:
> > > > > > > > Warning message:
> > > > > > > > In qnorm(psn.new <http://psn.new>) : NaNs produced
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > And when I tried to plot it
> > > > > > > > ggplot()  +
> > > > > > > >   gg(boundary)+gg(predjoint, mapping = aes_string(fill = "mean"))+colsc(predjoint$mean)
> > > > > > > >  I get just the map with the points and the posterior mean close to 1
> > > > > > > > > summary(predjoint$mean)
> > > > > > > >    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.
> > > > > > > >  0.9869  1.0000  1.0000  1.0000  1.0000  1.0000
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > El jueves, 30 de septiembre de 2021 a las 20:51:56 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> > > > > > > > > Hi again,
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > I got confused earlier; the problem with the predict() call is that you're supplying only a grid of coordinates (masked by the boundary), which is enough to predict the spatial fields, but to predict the given expression across space, you also need to supply the covariate values for all those grid points.
> > > > > > > > > The error message in this case comes when it tries to evaluate the "density" covariate and doesn't find it in the data set, and therefore ends up (due to R features...) finding the _function_ called density, and gets a NULL result. Before it gets to this point, it also tries to evaluate the DC and GDP covariates, but since their names don't appear anywhere at all, they get replaced by 1, for backwards compatibility reasons (I'm looking for a way to make this an error in a safe way, to make it easier to spot this kind of problem).
> > > > > > > > > So the error wasn't quite the same as the one as before, and it was instead a problem with the input data to predict().
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > Finn
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > On Thu, 30 Sept 2021 at 16:36, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > > > > Hi,
> > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > that looks like the same bug, but now only triggered by the predic call; good news is that this makes it easier to debug (and hopefully fix) !
> > > > > > > > > > I'll take a look. Your code looks like the way it's intended to work, so it's definitely a bug.
> > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > Finn
> > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > On Thu, 30 Sept 2021 at 16:31, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > > > > > Thanks Finn,
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > yes I have had the same issue with the include/exclude, after the second iteration stopped.
> > > > > > > > > > > I am trying to predict and the problem persists, probably because I am doing something wrong.
> > > > > > > > > > > Please, could you help me with this part?
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > df <- pixels(mesh, mask = boundary)
> > > > > > > > > > > pred_FULL <- predict(fit,
> > > > > > > > > > >                     df,
> > > > > > > > > > >                      ~ data.frame(lambda = plogis(Inter_mark +
> > > > > > > > > > >                                                     point_field_copy +
> > > > > > > > > > >                                                     mark_field +
> > > > > > > > > > >                                                     DC+GDP+density+total+rural+agriculture+industry +services +forest)),
> > > > > > > > > > >                      n.samples = 1000)
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > Error in row_kron(A[[k + 1]], A_) :
> > > > > > > > > > >   Size mismatch for row.kron, (n1, n2) = (1, 0)
> > > > > > > > > > > In addition: Warning message:
> > > > > > > > > > > In qnorm(psn.new <http://psn.new>) : NaNs produced
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > Thanks and sorry ineptness
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > Oscar
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > El jueves, 30 de septiembre de 2021 a las 11:17:14 UTC+1, finn.l...@gmail.com escribió:
> > > > > > > > > > > > Hi,
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > I had to change the mesh construction to a more reasonable size to debug this, but have found the main cause of the problem, which is a bug in how it handles "missing" data, which in this case means model components that are used by only one of the two likelihoods. The intended solution to this problem is to use the include/exclude arguments to like(), an if the models had been purely linear, this would work:
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > lik1 <- like("cp",
> > > > > > > > > > > >              formula = coordinates ~ .,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              data = data,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              ips = ips,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              include = c("point_field", "Inter_point"),
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > >              )
> > > > > > > > > > > > lik2 <- like("normal",
> > > > > > > > > > > >              formula = size_ha ~ .
> > > > > > > > > > > >              data = data,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              exclude = c("Inter_point"))
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > Unfortunately, the point_field*scaling term of the second predictor of your model means one has to specify the full expression in the formula, and then the bug is again triggered, in the second INLA iteration.
> > > > > > > > > > > > I'm not yet sure why the bug is triggered in the second step but not the first, so I need to debug some more to find out.
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > Fortunately, there is a (partly better) alternative, which is to use the INLA "copy" feature to implement the point_field*scaling term.
> > > > > > > > > > > > Use the following model specification:
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > cmp =
> > > > > > > > > > > >   ~ -1 +
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > >   point_field(coordinates, model = matern) +
> > > > > > > > > > > >   mark_field(coordinates, model = matern) +
> > > > > > > > > > > >   Inter_point(1) + Inter_mark(1) +
> > > > > > > > > > > >   point_field_copy(copy = "point_field", fixed = FALSE) +
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > >   DC+GDP+density+total+rural+agriculture+industry +services +forest
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > lik1 <- like("cp",
> > > > > > > > > > > >              formula = coordinates ~ .,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              data = data,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              ips = ips,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              include = c("point_field", "Inter_point"),
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > >              )
> > > > > > > > > > > > lik2 <- like("normal",
> > > > > > > > > > > >              formula = size_ha ~ .,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              data = data,
> > > > > > > > > > > >              exclude = c("point_field", "Inter_point"))
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > I've attached the full working code, including my modified mesh code suggestion that extends the mesh beyond the coastlines and artificial country borders, but sets up the likelihood only on the country bounded domain, just as before.
> > > > > > > > > > > > The mesh has far fewer nodes, making the inference take about 20 seconds, so you could still increase the resolution if you prefer.
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > Finn
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > On Mon, 27 Sept 2021 at 21:33, Oscar rodriguez de rivera ortega <oscarrodrig...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > > > > > > > Hi,
> > > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > > I am trying to build a spatial marked point process following the koala example.
> > > > > > > > > > > > > I am able to add one covariate to the mark, but when I am trying to add more I have the following error:
> > > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > > >ips <- ipoints(domain = mesh, samplers =boundary)
> > > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > > >matern <- inla.spde2.pcmatern(mesh,
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +                              prior.range = c(0.2, 0.01),
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +                              prior.sigma = c(2, 0.01))
> > > > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > > > > cmp =
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +   ~ -1 +
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +   point_field(coordinates, model = matern) +
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +   mark_field(coordinates, model = matern) +
> > > > > > > > > > > > > +   Inter_point(1) + Inter_mark(1) + scaling(1) +
